BS: By the owner? He just came in the morning of the horse and was telling who is going where. No [UNCLEAR] him, just the [? economists. ?] And so we have no saw him at all. He was a-- he was a Graf, [? Georg ?] [? von ?] [? Collin. ?] This is a Graf. [? Georg ?] [? von ?] [? Collin. ?] Yes, this place was calling Rittergut, [? Georg ?] Collin, was a Rittergut. Was-- it was his Gut. "Gut" is "estate," I think, this. Well, and so it was still April--

April '45. [PAUSES] Try to not starving, you know? And often, my sister didn't eat her bread. She's giving me her bread. And because I was growing, I was always hungry. And when front, Soviet front-- because this is East German, where I was-- when Soviet front is coming closer, [PAUSES] they took all of us from the Rittergut and put us in the first line, that the Soviet, when they shot to the German military, they shot us, and they can-- And so we have been through forests, fields from about 26 April till May 3. May 3, the Soviet free us, May 3, '45, 1945. And when they-- were-- they have been terrible, the Soviets also, special to the women. They raped all womens. And when they say you are not-- I am not German, the woman say, German, Polish, French, nothing count. You know? And my sister was doing everything to save me, and she saved me, thanks God. And she always I was-- saw that she covered. She was in the front, and she covered me. And then the Soviet, we want to go back. We like to go back to Poland. Try to go, but daytime was no allowed to go. Because, daytime, moved the military in the-- to Berlin, because this was close to Berlin. And we can go just nighttime this way, this way. And so we go nights. Days, we sleep in the forests, try to find some food in the villages, which was the German put out. We find some food, some clothes. And so we go, and when we get free May 3, we have been already in-- [PAUSES] from May 3-- from May 3, we have been May 8 by Stargard Szczecinski, and was the village Pezino. But was time that was supposed to be Poland. But the border change whole time. Poland-- Soviet-- Soviet is German. Poland is German. In this same time are coming people from East Poland with [? trains ?] where they must put-- the Soviet pushed them out. And on the way, they get cows, horses from UNRRA. Was United Nations, an American organization which give them that they can start farms on the new farms. But when the Soviet came-- when the Soviet came, they took away the UNRRA cows and horses. And there was a few times, and this was still January, '46. A few time changing. January 26 was a referendum, and this became Poland.

INT: Uh-huh. Can we go back just a little?

BS: Nobody believed that you can saw so terrible life through, though.

INT: OK, I just wanted to go back a little bit to the farm. So who--

BS: German farm?

INT: To the farm, yeah. Who were the other people with you on the farm?

BS: What?

INT: Who were the other women with you on the farm?

BS: How many other?

INT: Who were they?

BS: Was how many people working? On the farm? Was 50. We, womens, 16 womens, Polish. Then there was about 50 Polish Kriegsgefangener. It was soldiers, prison soldiers. Then it was about 150 Serbs, prisoners of the war, also. And was German. Was German, but mostly womens, because the mens was in the front. You know? And also, all their mens was working of the tractors, on the machines, you know? But-- and also Aufsichter. Aufsichter, the men must look that you must work.

INT: Did anybody ask-- [PAUSES] did anyone think you were Jewish

BS: On the farm? No, no, no, no, no, no. [LAUGHING] When I-- nobody recognized us. No. No, no. I was learning to pray Christian, Catholic prayers. And I pray with all the girls together. And I learned to-- also to sing the prayer songs. And the whole time, I was trying to learn this, that nobody recognized us. And I was-- you know, after the war also was, because when the Soviet-- we met some Soviet soldiers, which give some food and were Jewish. And we told him that we are Jewish and said, don't tell the Polish people. They will kill you. Don't tell them. And so we didn't tell that we are Jew. I didn't tell nobody. I go to school under false papers. And I went, and my sister get married also under false papers, playing that they are not Jewish. Then '51, when we need-- was I already work after school, then we make this first IDs after the war. And I need to have birth certificate, and I haven't, you know? Then, I must go. Then I decided. I told my one teacher that I am Jewish. I have no birth certificate. And she gave me money, and I went to Czestochowa for my birth certificate. But this is later, after school. It was last year. My Gymnasium, for diploma, I need the birth certificate for my diploma. Because I made first Gymnasium. And I must tell you after-- because after when-- you must go back. You must go back. Because when the Soviets stop us, then we work again in the fields. After referendum, September 1946, begins school, Polish school. And I went to the school. In this school, I make few grades in one year so that I finish the school June. In one year, I finished the school. June 1947 was the elementary school zusammen, together with middle school. And then, because I was-- I liked to learn, the director of the school [PAUSES] was-- with the-- make for me a stipendium that I can go to Gymnasium, to border school from this village, because there was just the middle school. I went to Walcz. This is about from this place where I live with my sister, about 150 kilometer. And there, I was in the school. I get-- we live eight girls in one room, and I get education there. I learn. I like to learn. I like-- I learn good. They give me a lot of premiums. You know, a shoe or underwear or something, because nothing-- I have nothing and also was very hard. And for all people was very hard. And when I finished this school-- by finishing this school, I came out to my teacher, just to my teacher, that I am Jewish. And then I went to Czestochowa. And Czestochowa, in the government office, statistic office, when I went for my birth certificate, I came to the man working there. And I said, I need birth certificate. My name is Fajgla Horowicz, daughter of Boruch Leib. And he said, no, you don't live. I said, I live. I stay here. He said, no, your siblings, Miriam and Moishe survived. But you and Ester no, and Frejima, no. I said, no, I survive. And I told him the whole-- He said, you know, your-- you came too late, two years too late. Because two years ago, your sister emigrated to Israel, and your brother emigrated already 1947 to Palestine. And they are now in Israel, because it was already Israel.

INT: Can I go back a little bit?

BS: But he has address. Because my sister-- my sister, after the war, her husband took back a factory, big factory. And he has one brother who was living in London. He said sent to him Garn, And he was making stockings and socks for men, because this was stocking-socks factory. And they have made very well after. I was hungry also at this time when they have everything. And they-- when they have problems with the communists, with the government, this man was helping them. And he-- after, when they decided that they must go so quick how possible, because this was beginning of very regiment. Was no more possibility to go out easy. They pay everything, and he was one who helped my sister. And she give him a lot stuff from her apartment. And also, she was writing. They have correspondence whole time. Then he sent telegram to my sister, "Miriam, I find your siblings."

INT: Can I go back? I just want to--

BS: Tel Aviv. You know, in this moment, all friends of my sister start to-- maybe our siblings are on life, you know? Because a big thing, the whole Czestochowa people, you know? But-- and-- I-- but it was not possible to go out from Poland. Already was closed. The border was closed. Was Stalinismus time. You no emigrate, no nothing. Was they are looking for Zionists, making all kind of process for the Zionists. Was terrible time. So then, I went back to Walcz with my papers. Also, I made already later-- he told me here, some people which know you are on life. Also, the owner of the house where I live, and Mrs. [? Winarowa, ?] he took me there. And yeah, I stay about two days. And I went back. And then, they made my papers. But also, I write a telegram to my sister that I find our siblings.

INT: Can I ask--

BS: She live in Pezino. In the-- it was state farm, in the state farm. Still living there. And then I have no enough money to go to tell her for the train. But later, the teacher give me money, some money. And I went to her to tell her, and we start to exchange [PAUSES] letters with Moishe, with Miriam. Then they tried to send us some packages, because we have no enough-- no enough clothes, no enough food, everything. And so was we have-- they tried to take us, me, because I have no family. I was just me. But it was not possible. Also through the Red Cross, through the Swiss Red Cross, was not possible to take me out. And so when I finished my school in Walcz, I went to Stettin. I became there-- I was--

INT: Can we just-- a couple of questions I have.

BS: What questions?

INT: To go back a little bit, during the war.

BS: During the war?

INT: Yeah. What was it like to leave your parents?

BS: What?

INT: What was it like to leave your parents?

BS: To leave my parents? Was terrible. Mama said, you go out. You are not allowed to look back. Go, She said to me. Daddy wasn't in-- was not home. Was Yom Kippur. She said, you go. Now, go. You are not allowed to cry, she said. You must learn Catechism. This is Catholic, to be Catholic. And don't cry! Till now, I cannot cry. You know what this mean? I have so terrible situation in my life, I never cry. I cannot. No, no [? tears. ?] Shh. You shut your [? tears. ?] This was it.

INT: And where were your siblings?

BS: What?

INT: Where were your siblings during this time?

BS: What?

INT: Where were your--

BS: My siblings? They have been home. They have been home. I went with Ester. Moishe, because it was-- you must know, was Polizei time. You are not allowed to go out after 6:00 from your apartment, also in ghetto. That's why my dad went before 6:00 to the shtiebel and stay whole night in the shtiebel. I think so. I don't know if they catch them. This-- I don't know what later was. I just remember he went to the shtiebel. And siblings have been all harmed. And Mama? Mama-- Frejma was supposed to go also with my older sister, but she said no, I stay with you. And she stayed with my parents. At night, Moishe and Miriam flew away also from the ghetto. Also was closed but through basement. Was basements under the houses. And because our house was close to the Polish part, they went through there out. And when they kill my parents, I don't know exactly, but all Czestochowa people know September 26-- 22, they put the Jews on trains and bring through to Treblinka to gas. And for us, the Yahrzeit is always September 22. And my sister went there, and my parents. Also, the whole Gold family, all Jews from Czestochowa. Just a little group they took for work on the river, regulation, river regulation. And they make a little ghetto. And from there, they took them to work.

INT: Where did Moishe and Miriam go?

BS: They came after us to Warsaw, the same way and back to Warsaw. Then, they stayed in Warsaw, and [? Antosiewicz ?] told that they can no help to stay as in Poland. They decide-- [? Marysia, ?] decide. Miriam, decide she's going back, trying to go back to the little ghetto, to [? Plazówka, ?] it's called, in [? Plazówka. ?] Because her boyfriend-- her boyfriend, [? Gutman, ?] was engineer, and he was working. They don't take him to Treblinka with the first transport. And Moishe said, she is not going by herself. I go with her. And so we-- But-- and they-- she worked on the [? Plazówka. ?] But they put her in HASAG. HASAG was a factory, ammunition factory in Czestochowa. She was working in HASAG. And he was going on the-- because he was [INAUDIBLE] engineer, he was working on the regulation on the Warta regulation. But one day, some people flew on the way back from the work. And he came with less-- he was no-- 15 people, was less. And they killed him. The German killed him. Also, and my sister survived HASAG. She was one from the 600 people who survived the concentration camp HASAG. And Moishe, after they kill [? Gutman, ?] ran away also next day. First to Mr. [? Wolf, ?] this man which has the factory from my father. And with help from him, he went to Arbeitsamt in Warsaw. He help him to make the papers. And also mister-- he went to work in Warsaw in slave work. But after 1 and 1/2 [? year, ?] somebody recognized him, and he must fly again inside, in Germany. But he fly, and then he went to the state Arbeitsamt there. In-- it was Mecklenburg-- in Mecklenburg. And say, I lost my papers. I don't know where, but I lost them. He made new papers with another names, and so he survived. He also gets free through the Soviets by Mecklenburg. But he straight ahead go back to Czestochowa. So he met my sister, and whole way to Eretz Yisrael.

INT: Where were you when you heard the war was over?

BS: What?

INT: Where were you when you heard the war was finished?

BS: I was by-- I was by Rostock. By Rostock, in the field. In the forest-- fields, forests. Because a [INAUDIBLE] came. The Russian [INAUDIBLE] came before us. And they said, stand up. No more German here. And we stand up. It was-- yeah.

INT: How did you react?

BS: We have to be happy that we are free from German, you know? But later was a hell again with the Soviet. Because they put on the front the [? convicts. ?] Terrible, you know, from [? convict ?] prisoners, all kinds. This was the last, you know, Berlin, storm of Berlin.

Yeah. [PAUSES] And again, a few months slave work. [PAUSES] You know, was so terrible when they took away the cows from the people, from the farmers, Polish farmers. And it was babies, and they milk the cows and take away-- we must-- the people must-- Polish people must milk. They took away the whole milk. They make from this [? sliwka, ?] and later, butter. And they made the whole milk without the fat. They put on the earth. No giving for the kids. Kids were-- babies are starving. This was-- you know or you have saw that the people make some people pass away, and they put on the graves. They make something, flowers. The ladies soldiers took this night from the graves.

INT: This was where?

BS: This is Pezino, Pezino by Stuttgart. But you have Stuttgart Szczecinski, because we have two Stuttgart, in Poland. Stargard. Polnisch is Stargard.

INT: And how did you get there?

BS: By foot. They stop us there. By foot. After front, over everything by foot.

INT: And how long were you there at the--

BS: In Pezino? September-- till September, 1947. Till I get to Walcz, to the school with internat.

INT: And where was your sister?

BS: What?

INT: Where was your sister?

BS: In Pezino, working. She was good in sewing. And she-- from the German, she took a sewing machine. And everybody needs sewing. And she was sewing, and they pay us with food. You know? So. And yeah. And later, she met her husband, not Jewish. And she-- he was from White Russia, but he ran away because he was also slave workers in Germany, by Königsberg. And the Stalin time, they took all slave workers and put in trains to Siberia, Siberia, to gulag. And he ran away by Stuttgart-- Stargard in [? Wolfino. ?] And so they-- he came to work there, and they met and fell in love. Was his first-- her first love. She'd never been in love.

INT: What was his name?

BS: [? Sofia. ?]

INT: His name.

BS: Ester. His name? [? Josef, ?] [? Josef ?] [? Romanczuk ?] And they emigrated. In 1968, they emigrated to Israel after the Gomulka, March 1868-- [PAUSES] 1968. And they have four children-- two boys and two girls. The boys went to military, Israeli military. One stay whole life in the military till pension, working for military Rafael. This is by Haifa, a military factory. And another one work also for military, but he was optic engineer. And he-- they have special kind for borders. [PAUSES] And they built this on the old borders all over. He was going as engineer, built this all over. Also, you know, German borders, French borders, from this, also Israeli patent. [PAUSES] And now they are both retired.

And yeah. And their daughter was nurse. And then she passed four years ago on cancer. And the youngest daughter is working. She finished the art school. But on art, you cannot make money, and she needs to make money for living. And she went to bank school, and she was working. Two years ago, she was retired bank. And they have all children, and they have grandchildren and great-grandchildren already. And, you know-- no, no great-grandchildren. Yeah. We have-- I have a lot, a lot nephews. I have the nephews. I have the kids of the nephews. One nephew has 13 grandchildren. He was alone, one son by my older sister, because she was very sick. After HASAG was-- you know, was that she could get one child was a wonder, you know? But he get four kids. And he has 13 grandchildren already.

I have a very big family in Israel. All my nephews has all has three or four children, and they have grandchildren.

It was time. What did-- the people in Israel try to have a lot-- you know, the Ashkenazi try to have each family, minimum, three. You know?

INT: And so for you, you finished school?

BS: After I finished school, I went to Stettin. And I became-- have a job. And I have qualified for teaching. Because after the war was no enough teachers. After Gymnasium, I made courses, and I can teach children from one to four. And I began by teaching. I became a little room for me in so-- in a place where four teachers living. And then I became a scout leader. I love this work. And I was-- later, I was making, you know, camps for 160 girls, as the leader, in summer camps. And I-- because I was always trying to sell my summer time that I get some money to boot-- to buy shoes or underwear or something, too, because-- And then also, I became very good scout leader. Good, good. They like me. I make a lot of good job with Scout groups after school. And then we begin to work with kids and other kids, not Scout kids, all kind of after-school activities. And we make a lot after-school activities for the kids. And so was with-- I work a lot, a lot, many hours-- a lot. I need zloty. Try to have a little bit more. And I also try-- you know, my sister has very hard time. I try to help her a little bit. And so until '90, December '93-- 1953, in this became-- I was already as Jewish. And I make first papers, my papers I'm Fajgla Horowicz, not changing to Barbara Sliwinska, just Fajgla Horowicz, Barbara Sliwinska. And most the time, we became a wave antisemitism. And they call this anti-Zionism. And they try-- they closed the Jewish school in Stettin. They closed everything what is possible. Yeah, and they put away the people from jobs, Jewish people. And they say, that me-- we try to make Zionist uprising. Idiots! But in any case, because I was very active, no active Zionistisch-- active for the kids, I must fly. In December, somebody told me, Barbara, you must fly from Stettin. because they put you and make a process for you. And this time, when I was so active, I was going to a lot courses which make the main quarter of Girl Scouts-- Scouts, not just Girl Scouts. And for the Girl Scouts was responsible [? Lokadia-- ?] now went away. My-- [? Lewinska, ?] [? Lokadia ?] [? Lewinska. ?] And she likes me very much. She put me all courses. She has a She invited me when I was there. Then I fly to her. I have her address. Whenever happens, she say, go. Just take train, come to me. And I stay by her few weeks. And also, helping-- I cannot get a job because they then registered where I am. There also was another scout leader, [? Janina ?] [? Balcerzak. ?] She helps also. And then they make me put me to live with I-- one lady, Krystyna Dabrowska, which was-- look, after the war, I must still fly. Krystyna Dabrowska, which was niece of a very famous composer, Karol Szymanowski. And she get the all author rights. And for this time, she was rich. She has a nice apartment because of him. And he passed away, but she has already-- she was the only person, one person, which hosts all right-- music rights for his. And he was a lot playing. His music was concerto music and King Roger opera music and all over the world. And she bought me a lot beautiful clothes, because I ran with nothing, again with nothing. She take care of me. She was journalist in a newspaper, week paper, around the world for use. She give me there a job with letters from the readers. And I prepared for her the letters, sort everything they got. And so I work more than a year. In April '55, the Ministry of Education start to prepare a big place, house, for work with kids after school, with street kids, for all kind of kids, and also for kids, regular, for regular family. And organizer of this was also Jerzy Berek. ?] He was for the headquarter Scout, the Scouts. And he took me to work for him. And I prepare with him the opening of the big project, very big project. We get-- we get a building with swimming pool, with gymnastic rooms, with technical rooms, with art rooms, theater, little theater, music rooms, with a concerto hall. And the opening was July 22, 1955. And I worked there long. I worked there till 1966-- '66. And we make performances for kids. And this for another time, this is-- this building is culture palace in middle in town of Warsaw. On another side is a Congress Hall, big Congress Hall with state. And we start to make performances for kids for Christmas, for Kids Day in June. We-- and because we make this, and I work-- start to work with professional artists. We make a lot performances. Maybe for Christmas, we begin with five and we finish with 32, two times a day. [BACKGROUND NOISE] What is it? [PAUSES 5 SECONDS] The door? [BACKGROUND NOISE] Come-- somebody there?

CREW: Let's move along. It was your--

BS: The lady? My God, idiot. [PAUSES] Is she gone?

CREW: She's gone.

BS: She's so loud.

And then we start to make performances for adult, with the artists. I was very good organizer, responsible, never counting time. From early morning, till it's late evening was doing this. And we performed in the Congress Hall. We could make money. And then, when we start to do it, I get an offer to work for the state agency Warsaw. And I left. the youth and I-- Estrada Stoleczna Warszawa. And I-- we prepare with another people, with directors, music director, and musicians, performances. We-- and we also go with the performances to another towns in Poland. And I was this one which organized hotel, transportation, stage wear. So. And so was my work. Big performances was my work till the end, till I go. Because first, I went with the Warsaw agency. Then I worked with the PAGART, a Polish state artist agency. I have here some prospects. You can look at them. And then, they give me to prepare Polish days in another countries, socialist countries. But in Russia, I went on tour with the orchestra with six weeks, with Polish program-- two pair dancers, soloist from the Opera House, Warsaw, and six singers. We make, build a program, estrada program, stage program. And we went, begin in Moscow. We went to Saint Petersburg, to Stalingrad. We went Ashgabat, Dushanbe, Novosibirsk, Irkutsk, Ulaanbaatar. Six weeks, this was my first tour, abroad tour with the orchestra and this. We came back. And this went very well, very well. Then, later, they give me to make a tour with Mazowsze. It's Polish ensemble dance like Moiseyev in Russland, Russia. Was Polish ensemble, Mazowsze. And we went on tour with Mazowsze to Russia, also. Then I get-- it was 1974 [PAUSES] was soccer mastership, Weltmeister cup in Frankfurt, Germany. And each country, 16 countries make a program. Poland was part on this 16 group, soccer group. And we have to make a program opening with the concept, also. Mr. [? Fletcher ?] was the main director that on the forest stadium, Waldstadion Hamburg-- Frankfurt am Main, is open balls, like soccer balls. And we have 15 minutes to show. Each country present. And they give me this job to make-- to organize ourselves. Was a director, but to organize our contract with the artists and so. And when we have first meeting with the concept, to refer the concept was [PAUSES] January '74. I ask for a dolmetscher, translator. Because after the war, I was no using German. I say, I will no use in my life German. At the event was a conference. Everybody has to. And they give me the translator. And I begin. And he was a bad translator. And I said to them, halt! I will tell this. You are a bad translator. And I tried tell in German. Then Mr. [? Fletcher ?] said, what? You told that you need translator. You do not get translator. And so I begin to work with German. No German artists. And I begin to go with myself to Germany, make performances there. And then they give me-- because this opening night was a big success. In Poland, get a bronze medal, and we must make-- the closing was in Stuttgart, and we make a closing program on the stage. And I organized this too. Then they give me to make Polish culture day 1976, and first Polish culture days in Essen, Germany. And we make the program. On the program was Orchestra, Symphony Orchestra, Cantor Theater experience-- very good theater. It was a ballet. We have ballet. Very good modern ballet. Drzewiecki, Poznán Dance Theater, and also jazz groups. And we make 10 days program in Essen. To Essen came mayors from another towns. This was the first Polish day in Essen. Was begin the time when [? Gehrig ?] was already on the [? beginning ?] contact. The Cold War was opening. Was-- you know, was-- they need money. With money, they open the culture to the West. And in this year, Poland has very good theater, drama theater, very good, very good. And we start to make exchange with theaters-- Thalia Theater, Hamburg, [PAUSES] Deutsches Theater in Göttingen, [PAUSES 5 SECONDS] Kammerspiele Theater München. [PAUSES 5 SECONDS] And what else? Another theater-- Hanover Theater. And Polish theater are coming with very good drama artists. And these are coming to us. And it was first theaters coming, and also we make exchange directors.

Heyme from Koln came to make direction. Holoubek went there to make. And this then was so that we make in Poland, 1978, a festival, theater festival. You have the international theater institute all over the world. And this institute make in every year another country has this festival. And we have it in Warsaw. And I was organizing this, everything. Then I met another from Roma theater, Roma theater. We bring Piccolo Theater. I get very good contacts with all directors. And directors, and they make exchange. Also, I bring-- we bring Russian theater, Tovstonogov Theater, Lyubimov Theater. This, too, theater we bring to Warsaw, because it must be modern theater, and they have been already the modern theater. And I bring Vysotsky for concerts. Last concert, in May-- the year, I don't remember which May, which year this moment. He passed away, but last concert, he make 7 July in Warsaw. And then he went back, and he passed away.

Yeah. Then very much helped me, this work in the state agency later, to build my own agency. You know? Because I worked with the state agency a lot. And because I was Jewish, I have all these problems sometimes, always, afraid of always if I get the passport this time. Because after, too, you must give the passport back. And also, if I get the job, you didn't know. Was enough people which like the job. But this way, I must always wish more show that I am good. And I have good-- I have luck, good contact to people. I love people. I love people, and the people likes me.

